Keep the IPsec tunnels across a P-CSCF restart that keeps the network namespace.

A restarted P-CSCF preloads its contacts from the database and believes every
UE is still registered, but it can no longer talk to any of them. The kernel
SAs are gone: ipsec_cleanall() flushes them in mod_init and again in
mod_destroy, so even a restart inside the same namespace - a container restart
in a pod that keeps its sandbox - starts empty. And the contacts come back
without their tunnels: ims_ipsec_pcscf writes both of them through - the one on
offer to the t_* columns, the one the UE registered over to the unprefixed
ones - but preload_udomain() selects none of those columns. Every request the
UEs send over ESP is dropped by the kernel (XfrmInNoStates) until they
re-register, and nothing tells them to.

In the same namespace the tunnels need not go anywhere, so keep them:

- ims_usrloc_pcscf: preload_udomain() reads the 15 t_* columns back into
  security_temp and the unprefixed ones into security. That set has no
  protocol column of its own ("protocol" is the contact's transport), so prot
  is left empty; the tunnel is always esp. The writers now store r_ealg/r_alg -
  the algorithms the UE offered and the tunnel is built with - in the ealg/ialg
  columns of both sets; they stored ealg/alg, which nothing ever sets, so those
  columns were always empty. A new walk_pcontacts() API visits every in-memory
  contact under its slot lock.
- ims_ipsec_pcscf: ipsec_preserve_tunnels (default 0) skips the flush at
  startup and shutdown. In the PROC_SIPINIT child, after the preload,
  ipsec_restore_tunnels() removes the tunnels no preloaded contact accounts for
  (delete_unused_tunnels(), or ipsec_cleanall() when no contact holds one),
  then marks the SPI pair of each tunnel a contact holds used - new
  reserve_spi() - and subscribes the contact for its expiry, as ipsec_create()
  would have. Without that subscription an expiring contact would leave its SAs
  in the kernel and its pair used forever.

The orphans go first because delete_unused_tunnels() releases a pair for every
SA it removes - the last contact's pair from its comparison loop, not the SA's -
and with nothing reserved yet that release has nothing to return. reserve_spi()
checks spi_remove() for 1: it returns -1 for a pair that is not in a non-empty
list, which release_spi() takes for success.

delete_unused_sa() and delete_unused_policy() no longer send an empty delete
batch when everything installed is accounted for; the kernel refuses it and the
restore logged two errors on every clean restart.

A new namespace still starts empty and the contacts then hold tunnels the kernel
does not; patch 0016 installs them again from what was preloaded.

